al"},{"text":"irinotecan","id":"C051890","label":"Chemical"},{"text":"hypertension","id":"D006973","label":"Disease"}],"head":"irinotecan","head_id":"C051890","pairs":[{"head":"contrast","tail":"nephropathy","head_id":"D003287","tail_id":"D007674"},{"head":"contrast","tail":"cancer","head_id":"D003287","tail_id":"D009369"},{"head":"contrast","tail":"acute renal failure","head_id":"D003287","tail_id":"D058186"},{"head":"contrast","tail":"hypertension","head_id":"D003287","tail_id":"D006973"},{"head":"creatinine","tail":"nephropathy","head_id":"D003404","tail_id":"D007674"},{"head":"creatinine","tail":"cancer","head_id":"D003404","tail_id":"D009369"},{"head":"creatinine","tail":"acute renal failure","head_id":"D003404","tail_id":"D058186"},{"head":"creatinine","tail":"hypertension","head_id":"D003404","tail_id":"D006973"},{"head":"Cr","tail":"nephropathy","head_id":"D002857","tail_id":"D007674"},{"head":"Cr","tail":"cancer","head_id":"D002857","tail_id":"D009369"},{"head":"Cr","tail":"acute renal failure","head_id":"D002857","tail_id":"D058186"},{"head":"Cr","tail":"hypertension","head_id":"D002857","tail_id":"D006973"},{"head":"bevacizumab","tail":"nephropathy","head_id":"-1","tail_id":"D007674"},{"head":"bevacizumab","tail":"cancer","head_id":"-1","tail_id":"D009369"},{"head":"bevacizumab","tail":"acute renal failure","head_id":"-1","tail_id":"D058186"},{"head":"bevacizumab","tail":"hypertension","head_id":"-1","tail_id":"D006973"},{"head":"irinotecan","tail":"nephropathy","head_id":"C051890","tail_id":"D007674"},{"head":"irinotecan","tail":"cancer","head_id":"C051890","tail_id":"D009369"},{"head":"irinotecan","tail":"acute renal failure","head_id":"C051890","tail_id":"D058186"},{"head":"irinotecan","tail":"hypertension","head_id":"C051890","tail_id":"D006973"}],"relations":["D003287 | D007674","D003287 | D058186","D002857 | D007674","D002857 | D058186","-1 | D007674","-1 | D058186","-1 | D006973","C051890 | D007674","C051890 | D058186","C051890 | D006973"],"result":"$","tail":"hypertension","tail_id":"D006973","text":"Incidence of contrast-induced nephropathy in hospitalised patients with cancer. OBJECTIVES: To determine the frequency of and possible factors related to contrast-induced nephropathy (CIN) in hospitalised patients with cancer. METHODS: Ninety adult patients were enrolled. Patients with risk factors for acute renal failure were excluded. Blood samples were examined the day before contrast-enhanced computed tomography (CT) and serially for 3 days thereafter. CIN was defined as an increase in serum creatinine (Cr) of 0.5 mg/dl or more, or elevation of Cr to 25 % over baseline. Relationships between CIN and possible risk factors were investigated. RESULTS: CIN was detected in 18/90 (20 %) patients. CIN developed in 25.5 % patients who underwent chemotherapy and in 11 % patients who did not (P = 0.1). CIN more frequently developed in patients who had undergone CT within 45 days after the last chemotherapy (P = 0.005); it was also an independent risk factor (P = 0.017). CIN was significantly more after treatment with bevacizumab/irinotecan (P = 0.021) and in patients with hypertension (P = 0.044). CONCLUSIONS: The incidence of CIN after CT in hospitalised oncological patients was 20 %. CIN developed 4.5-times more frequently in patients with cancer who had undergone recent chemotherapy. Hypertension and the combination of bevacizumab/irinotecan may be additional risk factors for CIN development. KEY POINTS: . Contrast-induced nephropathy (CIN) is a concern for oncological patients undergoing CT. . CIN occurs more often when CT is performed <45 days after chemotherapy. . Hypertension and treatment with bevacizumab appear to be additional risk factors.","metrics":{"precision":0.2,"recall":1.0,"f1":0.3333333333333333,"rel_tp":2,"rel_fp":8,"rel_fn":0}}